<body>
    <div class="container">
        <!-- HEADER -->
        <div class="header">
            <h1>Personal Tax Analysis FY 2025-26</h1>
            <p><strong>Name:</strong> Jeff Hall</p>
            <p><strong>Period:</strong> 1 July 2025 – 30 June 2026</p>
            <div class="metadata">
                <div><strong>Prepared:</strong> 2 August 2026</div>
                <div><strong>Status:</strong> Pre-Accountant Review</div>
                <div><strong>Risk Level:</strong> High (4 jurisdictions, ESS, Trust, Crypto)</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <!-- CONTENT -->
        <div class="content">

            <!-- EXECUTIVE SUMMARY -->
            <div class="section">
                <div class="section-title">Executive Summary</div>

                <div class="key-point">
                    <div class="key-point-icon">⚠</div>
                    <div class="key-point-content">
                        <strong>This return is high-complexity and high-risk.</strong> Four tax jurisdictions (Australia, Japan, USA, and decentralized networks), residency boundary-crossing, deferred-tax ESS with split vesting, non-resident trust distributions, and cryptocurrency rug pulls are all present. Professional accountancy is non-negotiable.
                    </div>
                </div>

                <p style="margin-top: 20px;">Your tax position involves:</p>
                <ul style="margin-left: 20px; margin-top: 10px;">
                    <li>Relocation from Brisbane to Tokyo (1 December 2025)</li>
                    <li>Salary in two currencies: AUD $250,000 (resident period) + ¥15,000,000 (non-resident period)</li>
                    <li>50,000 performance rights vesting in two tranches (Oct 2025 resident, April 2026 non-resident)</li>
                    <li>Trust distribution of $100,000 capital gains + $50,000 foreign income, with cash redirected to a company</li>
                    <li>DeFi liquidity pool rug pull (95% loss) and meme coin recovery (100% gain)</li>
                </ul>

                <div class="summary-grid" style="margin-top: 30px;">
                    <div class="summary-card">
                        <h4>Estimated Taxable Income</h4>
                        <p>$157,500–$200,000 AUD</p>
                        <p style="font-size: 12px; color: #666; margin-top: 10px; font-weight: normal;">Before offsets & deductions</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="summary-card">
                        <h4>Estimated Tax Liability</h4>
                        <p>$75,000–$95,000 AUD</p>
                        <p style="font-size: 12px; color: #666; margin-top: 10px; font-weight: normal;">Rough; depends on ESS & residency</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="alert-box">
                    <strong>Critical: Residency Determination is the Linchpin</strong><br><br>
                    If the ATO disputes your non-resident status from 1 December 2025, your entire ¥15 million Japanese salary (~AUD $150–160k) becomes assessable. This could increase your tax bill by ~$75,000. Get written advice from your accountant on this point before filing.
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- TIMELINE -->
            <div class="section">
                <div class="section-title">Key Dates & Events</div>
                <div class="timeline">
                    <div class="timeline-item">
                        <div class="timeline-date">1 July 2025 – 30 November 2025</div>
                        <div class="timeline-desc"><strong>Resident Period (Australian Salary)</strong> — You were resident in Australia on AUD $250,000 salary. Spouse & children also in Brisbane.</div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="timeline-item">
                        <div class="timeline-date">1 October 2025</div>
                        <div class="timeline-desc"><strong>ESS Vesting 1</strong> — 25,000 performance rights vest (market value assessable as income). You were resident.</div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="timeline-item">
                        <div class="timeline-date">1 December 2025</div>
                        <div class="timeline-desc"><strong>Relocation to Japan</strong> — 3-year lease, long-term work visa, local bank accounts opened. Likely non-resident from this date.</div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="timeline-item">
                        <div class="timeline-date">1 December 2025 – 30 June 2026</div>
                        <div class="timeline-desc"><strong>Non-Resident Period (Japanese Salary)</strong> — You earned ¥15 million from Japanese subsidiary. Foreign-source income, likely excluded from Australian tax.</div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="timeline-item">
                        <div class="timeline-date">February – April 2026</div>
                        <div class="timeline-desc"><strong>Return to Australia (20 days)</strong> — Board meetings in Brisbane. Spouse & children joined you in Tokyo on 1 April 2026.</div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="timeline-item">
                        <div class="timeline-date">1 April 2026</div>
                        <div class="timeline-desc"><strong>ESS Vesting 2</strong> — 25,000 performance rights vest (market value assessable). You were non-resident. [GREY AREA: May not be assessable if non-resident.]</div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="timeline-item">
                        <div class="timeline-date">March 2026</div>
                        <div class="timeline-desc"><strong>DeFi Rug Pull</strong> — Liquidity pool exploited; 95% loss on LP tokens. Meme coin acquired with remnants.</div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="timeline-item">
                        <div class="timeline-date">15 May 2026</div>
                        <div class="timeline-desc"><strong>ESS Sale</strong> — All 50,000 shares sold on NASDAQ. US backup withholding 30% applied due to incomplete tax paperwork.</div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="timeline-item">
                        <div class="timeline-date">28 June 2026</div>
                        <div class="timeline-desc"><strong>Trust Distribution</strong> — Trustee resolved to distribute $100,000 capital gains + $50,000 foreign income. Cash paid to corporate beneficiary instead of directly to you.</div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="timeline-item">
                        <div class="timeline-date">30 June 2026</div>
                        <div class="timeline-desc"><strong>EOFY</strong> — Financial year ends. Meme coin value doubles but still held (unrealized gain). Must be valued at fair market value for CGT.</div>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="page-break"></div>

            <!-- SECTION 1: RESIDENCY -->
            <div class="section">
                <div class="section-title">1. Residency Status – The Foundation</div>

                <p>Residency determines whether foreign-source income is assessable. Under the Statutory Residency Test (SRT), you are resident if:</p>
                <ul style="margin-left: 20px; margin: 10px 0;">
                    <li>You are in Australia for 183+ days in the FY, <strong>or</strong></li>
                    <li>You have a permanent home in Australia and are in Australia for part of the year with the intention to reside</li>
                </ul>

                <div class="fact-box">
                    <span class="tag tag-fact">FACT</span>
                    <strong>Your Status: Non-resident from 1 December 2025</strong><br><br>
                    <strong>1 Jul – 30 Nov 2025:</strong> You were resident in Australia (salary on ordinary rates).<br><br>
                    <strong>1 Dec 2025 – 30 Jun 2026:</strong> You relocated to Japan with:
                    <ul style="margin-left: 20px; margin-top: 10px;">
                        <li>3-year lease (fixed commitment to reside in Japan)</li>
                        <li>Long-term work visa (Japanese government recognizes you as resident worker)</li>
                        <li>Local bank accounts (economic ties to Japan)</li>
                        <li>Only 20 days in Australia (Feb–Apr 2026)</li>
                        <li>Spouse & children departed Australia 1 April 2026</li>
                    </ul>
                    <strong>Conclusion:</strong> You fail the "in Australia 183+ days" test and likely fail the "permanent home + intention" test. Non-resident status from 1 Dec 2025 is defensible.
                </div>

                <div class="subsection-title">Tax Impact — Income Split</div>

                <table>
                    <thead>
                        <tr>
                            <th>Income Stream</th>
                            <th>Period</th>
                            <th>Status</th>
                            <th>Amount (AUD)</th>
                            <th>Assessable?</th>
                        </tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr>
                            <td>Australian Salary</td>
                            <td>1 Jul – 30 Nov 2025</td>
                            <td>Resident</td>
                            <td class="amount">$250,000</td>
                            <td><strong style="color: #1b5e20;">✓ YES</strong></td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td>Japanese Salary</td>
                            <td>1 Dec 2025 – 30 Jun 2026</td>
                            <td>Non-resident</td>
                            <td class="amount">~$150,000–$160,000</td>
                            <td><strong style="color: #1b5e20;">✗ NO (foreign-source)</strong></td>
                        </tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>

                <div class="risk-box">
                    <strong>🚩 Risk: Residency Dispute</strong><br><br>
                    The ATO may argue you remained resident based on:
                    <ul style="margin-left: 20px; margin-top: 10px;">
                        <li>Retention of the Brisbane family home (now rented)</li>
                        <li>Presence of spouse & children in Australia until 1 April</li>
                        <li>Active Australian bank account & health insurance</li>
                    </ul>
                    <strong>Impact if ATO wins:</strong> +AUD $150,000–$160,000 assessable income = +~AUD $75,000 tax liability.<br><br>
                    <strong>Mitigation:</strong> Get your accountant to submit a formal residency ruling request (pre-lodgement review) or advise in writing that non-resident status is supportable.
                </div>

                <div class="subsection-title">Japanese Salary — Currency Conversion</div>

                <div class="assumption-box" style="background: #ede7f6; border-left: 4px solid #512da8; padding: 15px; margin: 15px 0; border-radius: 4px;">
                    <span class="tag tag-assumption">ASSUMPTION</span>
                    <strong>You received ¥15 million and converted to AUD at the time of receipt (or hold it in yen).</strong><br><br>
                    The ATO taxes the AUD equivalent at the exchange rate on the date of receipt. If you still hold it in yen, you have an unrealized foreign exchange gain/loss at EOFY which is also assessable.<br><br>
                    <strong>Approximate conversion:</strong> ¥15,000,000 at 1.0–1.07 JPY:AUD (Dec 2025–Jun 2026 rates) = AUD $150,000–$160,000.
                </div>

                <p><strong>Action item for accountant:</strong> Provide payslips or statements showing the JPY-to-AUD conversion rate used on each payment date.</p>

            </div>

            <!-- SECTION 2: EMPLOYEE SHARE SCHEME -->
            <div class="section">
                <div class="section-title">2. Employee Share Scheme (ESS) – Vesting & Sale</div>

                <p>The ESS has three taxing points: (1) vesting (ordinary income), (2) sale (capital gain/loss), and (3) the US backup withholding (refundable if you file a US return).</p>

                <div class="subsection-title">Tranche 1: 25,000 Rights Vesting 1 October 2025</div>

                <div class="fact-box">
                    <span class="tag tag-fact">FACT</span>
                    <strong>Vesting Date:</strong> 1 October 2025 (you were resident, in Australia).<br>
                    <strong>Applicable Law:</strong> Division 83A–83B ITAA97 (resident employee ESS).<br><br>
                    <strong>Assessable Income:</strong> Market value of shares at vesting − any amount you paid.<br><br>
                    <strong>Assumption:</strong> You paid nothing for the performance rights (deferred-tax ESS).
                </div>

                <p style="margin-top: 15px;"><strong>Calculation (you must provide share price at vesting):</strong></p>
                <div style="background: #f9f9f9; padding: 15px; border-radius: 4px; margin: 10px 0; font-family: monospace; font-size: 13px;">
                    Market value at 1 Oct 2025 = [Share price] × 25,000 shares<br>
                    Less: Amount paid = $0<br>
                    <strong>Assessable income = [Share price × 25,000]</strong>
                </div>

                <p style="margin-top: 15px;"><strong>No CGT discount applies at vesting.</strong> This is ordinary employment income, taxed at your marginal rate.</p>

                <div class="subsection-title">Tranche 2: 25,000 Rights Vesting 1 April 2026</div>

                <div class="alert-box">
                    <span class="tag tag-estimate">ESTIMATE</span>
                    <strong>Vesting Date:</strong> 1 April 2026 (you were non-resident, in Japan).<br><br>
                    <strong>GREY AREA:</strong> Whether Division 83A applies to non-residents.
                </div>

                <p style="margin-top: 15px;">The law states that Div. 83A applies to "<em>Australian-resident employees.</em>" On 1 April 2026, you were non-resident. Two ATO positions are possible:</p>

                <ol style="margin-left: 20px;">
                    <li><strong>Position A (Strict):</strong> Because you were non-resident at vesting, Div. 83A does not apply. The shares are a capital asset acquired on 1 April 2026 at market value. No ordinary income is assessed. (The later CGT gain/loss is foreign-source if the US parent is the issuer.)</li>
                    <li><strong>Position B (Source-Based):</strong> Because the rights were originally granted while you were an Australian resident, and the employment contract was Australian-source, the gain at vesting is deemed Australian-source, and Div. 83A applies even though you vested as a non-resident. Assessable income = Market value at 1 April 2026.</li>
                </ol>

                <div class="risk-box">
                    <strong>🚩 Risk: ESS Second Tranche Treatment</strong><br><br>
                    The ATO has not issued clear guidance on this scenario. A tax agent familiar with international employment should advise on the likelihood of challenge. Position B is more aggressive but not uncommon in ATO practice.<br><br>
                    <strong>Mitigation:</strong> Keep documentation showing:
                    <ul style="margin-left: 20px; margin-top: 10px;">
                        <li>The grant was made when you were resident</li>
                        <li>The vesting schedule was set at grant (not modified later)</li>
                        <li>Your relocation to Japan occurred after vesting 1 was locked in</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>

                <div class="subsection-title">Capital Gain/Loss on Sale (15 May 2026)</div>

                <div class="fact-box">
                    <span class="tag tag-fact">FACT</span>
                    <strong>Sale Date:</strong> 15 May 2026 on NASDAQ.<br>
                    <strong>Holding Period:</strong> 
                    <ul style="margin-left: 20px; margin-top: 10px;">
                        <li>Tranche 1 (Oct 2025 → May 2026) = ~7.5 months</li>
                        <li>Tranche 2 (Apr 2026 → May 2026) = ~1.5 months</li>
                    </ul>
                    <strong>CGT Discount:</strong> <strong>NOT available</strong> (need 12+ months for 50% discount).<br><br>
                    <strong>Capital Gain/Loss Calculation:</strong><br>
                    <div style="background: #f9f9f9; padding: 10px; margin: 10px 0; font-family: monospace; font-size: 12px;">
                    Sale price (15 May 2026) − Vesting value (1 Oct 2025 & 1 Apr 2026) = Net CGT gain or loss
                    </div>
                </div>

                <p style="margin-top: 15px;"><strong>You must provide:</strong></p>
                <ul style="margin-left: 20px;">
                    <li>Share price on 1 October 2025 (Tranche 1 vesting)</li>
                    <li>Share price on 1 April 2026 (Tranche 2 vesting)</li>
                    <li>Sale price on 15 May 2026 (actual NASDAQ transaction price)</li>
                </ul>

                <div class="subsection-title">US Backup Withholding (30%)</div>

                <div class="fact-box">
                    <span class="tag tag-fact">FACT</span>
                    <strong>US Tax Issue (Not Australian Primary Issue):</strong> The US broker withheld 30% backup withholding because you did not provide a completed W-8BEN (non-resident alien certification).
                </div>

                <p style="margin-top: 15px;"><strong>Two-Step Recovery:</strong></p>

                <ol style="margin-left: 20px;">
                    <li><strong>US Return (Form 1040-NR):</strong> File a US non-resident alien return claiming the 30% as a refundable credit. Deadline is typically 3 years from sale date (so by 15 May 2029).</li>
                    <li><strong>Australian Foreign Tax Credit (FTC):</strong> Once you recover the US tax (or it's withheld), claim it as an FTC on your Australian return (schedule by amount of foreign-source income).</li>
                </ol>

                <div class="risk-box">
                    <strong>🚩 Risk: Missed US Filing</strong><br><br>
                    If you do not file Form 1040-NR, you lose the 30% refund permanently. The US IRS will not recover it automatically.<br><br>
                    <strong>Impact:</strong> ~30% × [ESS capital gain] lost forever.
                </div>

                <p style="margin-top: 15px;"><strong>Action item:</strong> Provide the NASDAQ sale statement and contact a US tax professional (CPA or enrolled agent) to file Form 1040-NR before 15 May 2029. Keep the 30% withholding receipt from the broker.</p>

            </div>

            <div class="page-break"></div>

            <!-- SECTION 3: TRUST DISTRIBUTION -->
            <div class="section">
                <div class="section-title">3. Trust Distribution & Section 100A – Payment Diversion Issue</div>

                <div class="alert-box">
                    <strong>Critical Issue:</strong> The trustee resolved to distribute income to you, but the cash was paid to a third-party company. This creates an ATO mismatch.
                </div>

                <div class="subsection-title">Distribution Details</div>

                <table>
                    <thead>
                        <tr>
                            <th>Component</th>
                            <th>Amount</th>
                            <th>Type</th>
                            <th>Taxable (You)</th>
                        </tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr>
                            <td>Capital gains (discounted)</td>
                            <td class="amount">$100,000</td>
                            <td>Australian shares (capital gain)</td>
                            <td class="amount">$50,000 (50% discount)</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td>Foreign-source income</td>
                            <td class="amount">$50,000</td>
                            <td>Untaxed foreign income (trust)</td>
                            <td class="amount">$0–$50,000 <strong>[DISPUTE]</strong></td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr style="font-weight: bold; background: #fff3cd;">
                            <td>Total Distribution</td>
                            <td class="amount">$150,000</td>
                            <td>–</td>
                            <td class="amount">$50,000–$100,000</td>
                        </tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>

                <div class="subsection-title">Section 100A Assessability</div>

                <div class="fact-box">
                    <span class="tag tag-fact">FACT</span>
                    <strong>Section 100A ITAA36 Rule:</strong> A beneficiary of a discretionary trust is assessed on the net income the trustee resolved to distribute to that beneficiary in the year, <strong>regardless of whether the cash was actually received</strong>. Payment location does not change assessability.
                </div>

